Phase 4: Kubernetes Deployment with Helm
Objective

The objective of this phase was to deploy the Dockerized NGINX application on a local Kubernetes cluster using Helm, enabling version-controlled deployments, simplified application management, rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling.

Commands Executed
1. Create Helm Chart
helm create nginx

Creates the basic Helm chart structure.

2. Validate the Helm Chart
helm lint nginx

Checks the Helm chart for syntax and configuration errors.

3. Preview Kubernetes Manifests
helm template nginx ./nginx

Renders the Kubernetes manifests without deploying them.

4. Install the Application
helm install nginx ./nginx

Deploys the application to the Kubernetes cluster.

5. Verify Deployment
kubectl get all

Displays all Kubernetes resources.

kubectl get pods

Lists running Pods.

kubectl get deployment

Displays Deployment details.

kubectl get svc

Shows Services.

kubectl get hpa

Displays Horizontal Pod Autoscaler status.

6. Build Docker Image
docker build -t agamtyagi/xqora-nginx:v2 .

Builds the application image.

7. Push Image to Docker Hub
docker push agamtyagi/xqora-nginx:v2

Uploads the image to Docker Hub for Kubernetes to pull.

8. Upgrade Application
helm upgrade nginx ./nginx

Applies configuration or image updates without reinstalling the application.

9. Check Rollout Status
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx

Monitors the progress of a rolling update.

10. View Rollout History
kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx

Displays previous deployment revisions.

11. Rollback Deployment
kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx

Reverts the Deployment to the previous revision.

12. View Helm Release History
helm history nginx

Displays all Helm release revisions.

13. Rollback Helm Release
helm rollback nginx 1

Restores the Helm release to revision 1.

14. Manual Scaling
kubectl scale deployment nginx --replicas=5

Increases the number of application Pods.

15. Verify Scaling
kubectl get pods

Confirms the updated number of running Pods.

Outcome
Successfully deployed the application on a local Kubernetes cluster using Helm.
Managed application deployments through Helm releases.
Implemented rolling updates for zero-downtime deployments.
Performed rollbacks using both Kubernetes and Helm.
Verified application health and deployment status.
Scaled the application manually using Kubernetes.
Used Docker Hub as the image registry for Kubernetes deployments.
Gained practical experience with Helm-based application lifecycle management. 


